{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SkewSYTBranchingBijection","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mathlib.SkewSYTBranchingBijection","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.SkewSYTMaxCorner"],"declarations":14,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.fDimSkew_branching","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.fDimSkew_branching","kind":"theorem","line":332,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SkewSYTBranchingBijection.lean#L332-L344","doc":"**The skew branching rule for `fDimSkew`.**\n\n    f^{outer/inner} = ∑_{c a corner of outer, c ∉ inner} f^{(outer ∖ c)/inner}.\n\nThe containment hypothesis is what lets the guard in `fDimSkew` be discharged on both sides:\n`le_eraseCorner_of_notMem` says deleting a corner outside `inner` keeps `inner` inside. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.card_skewSYT_branching","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.card_skewSYT_branching","kind":"theorem","line":321,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SkewSYTBranchingBijection.lean#L321-L330","doc":"**The skew branching rule, at the level of `Nat.card`.**  For a non-empty skew region,\n\n#SkewSYT(outer/inner) = ∑_{c a corner of outer, c ∉ inner} #SkewSYT((outer ∖ c)/inner). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skewBranchInv_injective","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skewBranchInv_injective","kind":"theorem","line":278,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SkewSYTBranchingBijection.lean#L278-L319","doc":"`skewBranchInv` is injective: the inserted corner is recovered as the unique cell carrying\nthe top value (both fibres put the *same* value there, since deleting any corner drops the skew\nsize by exactly one), and then roundtrip 2 pins the tableau. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skewBranchInv_surjective","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skewBranchInv_surjective","kind":"theorem","line":260,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SkewSYTBranchingBijection.lean#L260-L276","doc":"`skewBranchInv` is surjective: every skew tableau on a non-empty skew region is the\nre-insertion, at its maximal corner, of the deletion of that corner. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skewBranchInv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skewBranchInv","kind":"def","line":253,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SkewSYTBranchingBijection.lean#L253-L258","doc":"Re-insert the top value at the indexed corner. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.fintypeSkewCorners","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.fintypeSkewCorners","kind":"instance","line":245,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SkewSYTBranchingBijection.lean#L245-L251","doc":"The corners of `outer` lying outside `inner` form a finite type. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skewEraseMax_skewInsertMax","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skewEraseMax_skewInsertMax","kind":"theorem","line":224,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SkewSYTBranchingBijection.lean#L224-L241","doc":"**Roundtrip 2.**  Re-inserting the top value at `c` and deleting that corner recovers `S`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skewInsertMax_skewEraseMax","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skewInsertMax_skewEraseMax","kind":"theorem","line":203,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SkewSYTBranchingBijection.lean#L203-L222","doc":"**Roundtrip 1.**  Deleting the maximal corner and re-inserting the top value recovers `T`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.entry_skewInsertMax_corner","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.entry_skewInsertMax_corner","kind":"theorem","line":193,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SkewSYTBranchingBijection.lean#L193-L201","doc":"**The re-inserted tableau carries the top value at the corner.** ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skewEraseMax","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skewEraseMax","kind":"def","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SkewSYTBranchingBijection.lean#L93-L189","doc":"**Delete the maximal entry at a corner outside `inner`.**  The mirror of `skewInsertMax`:\nzero out the corner and keep `T` elsewhere.\n\n`content` is the only field that does real work.  Splitting `skew outer inner` as\n`insert c (skew (outer ∖ c) inner)` turns `T.content : image = range (n + 1)` into\n`insert n (image over the smaller region) = insert n (range n)`; since `n` is missing from both\ninner sets (from `skew_injOn`, the only cell carrying `n` is the deleted corner), erasing `n`\nfrom both sides gives exactly the smaller `content`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.finiteSkewSYT","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.finiteSkewSYT","kind":"instance","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SkewSYTBranchingBijection.lean#L72-L86","doc":"**There are finitely many skew standard tableaux of a given shape.**\n\n`fDimSkew` is defined as a `Nat.card`, and nothing in the corpus had yet established that this\ncount is finite — so every `Nat.card` fact about it was, strictly, a fact about a possibly-junk\n`0`.  The restriction map `T ↦ (entry on the skew cells, valued in `Fin |skew|`)` is injective by\n`skew_entry_ext` and lands in a finite type, which settles it. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skew_entry_ext","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skew_entry_ext","kind":"theorem","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SkewSYTBranchingBijection.lean#L61-L70","doc":"Two skew tableaux agreeing on the skew region agree everywhere: off it both are `0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.mem_skewCellsOf_of_corner","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.mem_skewCellsOf_of_corner","kind":"theorem","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SkewSYTBranchingBijection.lean#L55-L59","doc":"A corner outside `inner` is a skew cell. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skew_syt_ext","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.YoungTableauStandard.skew_syt_ext","kind":"theorem","line":46,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mathlib/SkewSYTBranchingBijection.lean#L46-L53","doc":"Two skew tableaux of the same shape are equal when their entry maps agree — the other four\nfields are `Prop`s, hence proof-irrelevant. ","signatureChunk":0}]}